findings.evidence:
Verbatim from src/model/transformer.py:
    def _init_weights(self):
        std = 0.02
        for module in self.modules():
            if isinstance(module, nn.Linear):
                nn.init.normal_(module.weight, mean=0.0, std=std)
            elif isinstance(module, nn.Embedding):
                nn.init.normal_(module.weight, mean=0.0, std=std)
No per-module fan-in scaling and no residual/depth scaling. Note also that down_proj has fan_in=3584, for which 1/sqrt(3584)=0.0167, while q_proj has fan_in=1280 → 1/sqrt(1280)=0.0280; a single flat 0.02 is not principled for either.

findings.claim:
ROOT CAUSE OF ~0% EXECUTABLE CODE: the Plasma 1.1 tokenizer physically cannot represent Python indentation. `scripts/train_tokenizer.py` never sets `remove_extra_whitespaces=False`, and SentencePiece defaults that flag to True, so every run of spaces collapses to a single space. Verified on the shipped `data/tokenizer_1.1.model`: encode→decode of `'class A:
    def f(self):
        if True:
            return 1
'` returns `'class A:
 def f(self):
 if True:
 return 1
'` — all four nesting levels flattened to one space. A generated function containing ANY nested block therefore raises IndentationError.

findings.evidence:
Executed locally: `sp.decode(sp.encode('class A:
	def f(self, n):
		if n > 0:
			return n
		return 0
')) == original` → True. Token stream shows `<0x0A>`, `<0x09>`, `<0x09>` repeated per indent level. The 4-space version of the same string round-trips False.
